<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Sacred · c. 80 · Ancient Near East</p><h1 class="sg-title">Magnificat</h1><p class="sg-local"><bdi lang="grc">Magnificat</bdi> / <bdi lang="grc-Latn">Ἡ ᾨδὴ τῆς Θεοτόκου (the Ode of the Theotokos)</bdi></p><p class="sg-kid" data-say>The song of praise that Mary sings in the Gospel of Luke. Churches have sung it every evening for many centuries.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>anonymous (in the Gospel of Luke, spoken by Mary)</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>traditional</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>Early Christians; later Catholic, Orthodox, Lutheran and Anglican worship</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Koine Greek</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>recorded in the Gospel of Luke (1:46–55)</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Sacred</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (traditional; a canticle of the Gospel of Luke)</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnificat">Magnificat</a>, also called the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> of Mary or Canticle of Mary, and in the Byzantine Rite the Ode of the Theotokos, is a canticle taken from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gospel-of-luke">Gospel of Luke</a> (1:46–55). In Luke's narrative Mary visits her cousin Elizabeth, who is pregnant with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-the-baptist">John the Baptist</a>; the child moves in Elizabeth's womb, Elizabeth praises Mary for her faith, and Mary answers with the Magnificat. Its Western name comes from the first word of its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> text. Some ancient authorities give the song to Elizabeth instead, and in 1900 Adolf von Harnack argued for that reading; Jeffrey Kloha notes that few commentaries and no edition of the Greek <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-testament">New Testament</a> of the last half-century agree, though he holds that the case can be made. Scholars also discuss whether the historical Mary herself spoke it.</p><p>It is one of four hymns in Luke's account of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a>'s infancy, with Zechariah's Benedictus, the angels' Gloria in Excelsis Deo and Simeon's Nunc dimittis, thought to be drawn from a collection of early Jewish-Christian canticles. In form they follow the hymns of praise of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-psalms">Psalter</a> and the Jewish hymn-writing of their time, built on the parallel lines of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> poetry. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnificat">Magnificat</a> echoes many passages of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew-bible">Hebrew Bible</a>, above all the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-of-hannah">Song of Hannah</a> in 1 Samuel. The oldest, probably original, version is in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koine-greek">Koine Greek</a>, and it is one of the songs gathered in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/classic-of-poetry">Book of Odes</a> found in some <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/septuagint">Septuagint</a>. It is counted among the eight most ancient Christian hymns and is perhaps the earliest hymn to Mary.</p><p>It became the most popular of the canticles. In the West it is sung or said at the main evening service, Vespers in the Catholic and Lutheran churches and Evening Prayer or Evensong in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglicanism">Anglican Communion</a>, where it is usually paired with the Nunc dimittis and followed by the Gloria Patri, a doxology not found in the original text. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Eastern Orthodox Church</a> it is sung at Matins, with a refrain to Mary between its verses. It is also read in Advent and, among Lutherans, at the Feast of the Visitation on 2 July.</p><p>Composers have set it since the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a>, among them Monteverdi in his Vespers of 1610, Vivaldi, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johann-sebastian-bach">Johann Sebastian Bach</a> (1723, revised 1733), <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anton-bruckner">Bruckner</a>, Rachmaninoff and Arvo Pärt. For the Anglican Evensong, where the &quot;Mag and Nunc&quot; are sung daily in some cathedrals, Charles Villiers Stanford wrote a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnificat">Magnificat</a> in every major key and Herbert Howells published 18 settings. Hymn paraphrases include Maria Luise Thurmair's German &quot;Den Herren will ich loben&quot; (1954), Timothy Dudley-Smith's &quot;Tell Out, My Soul&quot; (1962) and Rory Cooney's &quot;Canticle of the Turning&quot; (1990).</p><p>Its theme of the mighty brought down and the lowly lifted up has given it a political edge. In Nicaragua it is a favourite prayer of many peasants and is often carried as a sacramental; under the Somoza government, when campesinos had to carry proof that they had voted for Somoza, they mockingly called that document a &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnificat">Magnificat</a>&quot;. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-raj">British Raj</a>, Archbishop William Temple joked to the evangelist Jack Winslow, who sympathised with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-independence">Indian independence</a>, that singing the Magnificat would make him look even more of a revolutionary than his home-spun Indian clothes did.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A song of praise in which Mary rejoices in God for choosing her to bear the promised Messiah and glorifies his power, holiness and mercy. It looks forward to a world turned upside down, the proud humbled and the humble raised, the hungry fed and the rich sent away with nothing, and ends by praising God's faithfulness to his promise to Abraham. Its mood is joyful and confident.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-show" role="group" aria-label="Show these columns"><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="orig">Latin (the Vulgate)</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="en">In English</button></div><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-2"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">Latin (the Vulgate)</span><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Magníficat ánima mea Dóminum,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>My soul magnifies the Lord,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">et exsultávit spíritus meus</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>and my spirit has exulted</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">in Deo, salutári meo,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>in God, my saviour,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">quia respéxit humilitatem</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>because he has looked upon the lowliness</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">ancíllae suae.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>of his handmaid.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Ecce enim ex hoc beátam</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>For see, from this time blessed</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">me dicent omnes generatiónes,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>all generations will call me,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">quia fecit mihi magna,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>because he has done great things for me,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">qui potens est,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>he who is mighty,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">et sanctum nomen eius,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>and holy is his name,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">et misericórdia eius in progénies</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>and his mercy is unto generations</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">et progénies timéntibus eum.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>and generations, for those who fear him.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Fecit poténtiam in bráchio suo,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>He has worked might with his arm,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">dispérsit supérbos mente cordis sui;</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>he has scattered the proud in the mind of their heart;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">depósuit poténtes de sede</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>he has brought down the powerful from the seat</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">et exaltávit húmiles;</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>and has raised up the lowly;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">esuriéntes implévit bonis</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>the hungry he has filled with good things</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">et dívites dimísit inánes.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>and the rich he has sent away empty.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Suscépit Ísrael púerum suum,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>He has taken up Israel his servant,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">recordátus misericórdiae suæ,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>mindful of his mercy,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">sicut locútus est ad patres nostros,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>as he spoke to our fathers,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Ábraham et sémini eius in sæcula.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>to Abraham and to his seed for ever.</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: The Latin text of the Vulgate (Luke 1:46–55). Text from <a href="https://la.wikisource.org/wiki/Magnificat" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a>; public domain.</p><p>The English is the Institute&#8217;s own translation, made from the original for this page.</p></div><details class="sg-notes"><summary>Notes on the translation (2)</summary><ul><li>line 1.19: 'puerum' is 'child' or 'servant'.</li><li>line 1.11-12: 'in progenies et progenies', literally 'unto offspring and offspring', from generation to generation.</li></ul></details></section>
</div>
